course: dictionary
lesson: 15288
title: A grand word: singularly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Singularly is a formal word, so you will mostly meet it in old books and grown-up writing. Its main meaning is very, unusually or especially. «The old clock was singularly ugly.» The word tells you the clock was extremely ugly, more than most clocks.
explain: Use the context to check. In «The party was singularly quiet for a Saturday», the words «for a Saturday» hint that it was unusually quiet. Swap in very and see if the sentence still makes sense. It does, so very is a good meaning.
explain: Singularly can also mean one at a time or separately, but singly is the more common word for that. Maths uses singular to mean one, but singularly almost always means very, so do not mix them up.
fact: Singularly is an adverb, so it tells you more about an adjective or a verb.
fact: «Singularly» means very, unusually or especially.
fact: «Singularly» is mostly found in old books and formal writing.
fact: «Singularly» can also mean one at a time or separately, but «singly» is more common for this.
fact: In maths, «singular» means one, but «singularly» almost always means very.
exercise: «The soup was singularly bland.» What does singularly mean here? | Singing loudly | Quickly | Hot and spicy | *Very, in an unusual way
exercise: Which word could replace singularly in «a singularly kind man» without changing the meaning much? | sadly | *very | never | slowly
exercise: «The children crossed the bridge singly.» What does singly mean? | *One at a time | All together | Very happy | Very loudly
test: «The cake was singularly delicious.» What does singularly mean? | *Very, especially | Sadly | Hidden under a table | Slowly
test: Which word means one at a time, like singularly in its second meaning? | Cheerfully | *Singly | Loudly | Never
exam: «The view from the top was singularly beautiful, so we stayed for hours.» What does singularly mean here? | Slowly and quietly | *Very, especially | In a rude way | Only just
